    historyContent: {
      origins: 'Brag emerged in 16th-century England as a three-card vying game. It evolved into the parent of Indian Teen Patti and influenced the betting structures of early American Poker variants like Three-Card Poker.',
      originCountries: [
        'Great Britain',
      ],
      timeline: [
        "1500s: Early versions of 'Post and Pair' and Brag documented in England.",
        "1700s: The 'Bragger' (wild card) mechanic is introduced, usually the Jack of Clubs.",
        '1900s: Remains the standard gambling game in British military and social clubs.',
        'Present: A cultural staple of British pub life and high-stakes private games.',
      ],
      evolution: "The game's hand rankings (Prial > Running Flush > Run) and the half-price blind betting have remained largely consistent for centuries, despite the global dominance of Poker.",
      cultural: "Symbolizes 'working class' poker in the UK; featured in numerous British films like 'Lock, Stock and Two Smoking Barrels'.",
    },
