          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Win the pot by holding the best three-card combination or by being the last player remaining after others fold.',
          gameplay: "1. **Blind/See**: Players decide whether to bet 'Blind' (unseen) or 'See' their cards. 2. **Betting**: Blind players bet N; Seeing players must bet at least 2N. 3. **Folding**: A player can fold at any time, losing all previous contributions. 4. **Showdown**: If only two players remain, one can 'See' the other by paying double the current bet to compare hands. 5. **Hand Ranks**: Prial (Three of a Kind), Running Flush (Straight Flush), Run (Straight), Flush, Pair, High Card.",
          keyRules: [
            'Prial of 3s is the highest possible hand (3-3-3 beats A-A-A).',
            "A Blind player's hand beats a Seeing player's identical hand (Blind advantage).",
            "A Seeing player can only 'See' a player when only two people remain in the hand.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'Stay Blind as long as possible. Betting half-price allows you to build the pot while making it expensive for Seeing players to stay in with marginal hands.',
          intermediate: "The 'Prial' Hunt: If you hold a pair of 3s, you are statistically likely to have the best hand. If you hold a 3 and a wild card ('Bragger'), your hand is incredibly strong.",
          advanced: 'Range Inference: Watch the Betting Speed. A player who sees their cards and immediately raises is often representing a Run or better. A player who stays Blind for many rounds is either bluffing or has complete confidence in the shuffle.',
          tips: [
            "Never 'See' your cards too early; the psychological pressure of a Blind player is your best weapon.",
            "If you are a Seeing player, don't chase a Blind bet unless you have at least a Flush.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
